course: dictionary
lesson: 14723
title: Proffer: offering something politely
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Proffer is a formal word. It means to hold something out so that someone can take it, or to offer it. «The waiter proffered the menu with a small bow.» The waiter held the menu out for the guest to take, so proffer means offer.
explain: Proffer can also mean to offer advice, help or an explanation. «Mia proffered an explanation for the broken vase, but nobody believed her.» Mia offered a reason for the broken vase. The clue is that she gave an explanation, which is something you offer with words.
explain: When you meet proffer, look for the clues. Is something being held out or offered? Is the writing polite or old-fashioned? Then swap in the everyday word offer. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Proffer is a verb, and its forms are proffer, proffers, proffered and proffering.
fact: «Proffer» means to hold something out to someone so that they can take it.
fact: «Proffer» can also mean to offer advice, help or an explanation.
fact: «Proffer» is a formal word, and everyday writers often use offer instead.
fact: A good check is to swap offer into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The shopkeeper proffered a bag of apples to the little girl.» What does proffered mean? | Hidden under the counter | Counted carefully | Dropped on the floor | *Held out to be taken; offered
exercise: «Sam proffered his help when the team was stuck.» What does proffered mean here? | *Offered help | Hid his face | Ran away from the team | Lost the ball
exercise: Which everyday word means about the same as proffer? | whisper | *offer | jump | sleep
test: «The king proffered his hand to the kneeling knight.» What did the king do with his hand? | Put it in his pocket | *Held it out for the knight to take | Shook it hard in anger | Hid it behind his back
test: «Dad proffered an explanation for the late bus.» What does proffered mean? | Sang a song | Asked a question | Forgot all about it | *Offered an explanation
exam: Which word could replace proffer in «Grandma proffered some sensible advice»? | Lost | *Offered | Ignored | Shouted
